symptoms in many patients.3
About 5-8% of SPS cases are associated with malig-
nant tumors, and symptoms can resemble those of a
paraneoplastic condition.22 The most frequent compli-
cating malignancy is breast cancer, but thyroid, small
cell lung, colon, and renal cell carcinomas, as well as lym-
phoma and thymoma, have also been reported. Antibod-
ies to amphiphysin are frequently detectable under
these conditions.©”? Several reports have described SPS
with thymoma, but this is quite rare, and the frequency
is unknown. A summary by Vernino and Lennon found
only one patient with thymoma or thymic carcinoma as-
sociated with SPS among 201 patients. To our knowl-
edge, only six other patients have undergone thymec-
tomy for SPS with thymoma or thymic carcinoma, and
